    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nThe `vim.version.range()` approach indeed came organically specifically during `vim.pack` development. As initial version assumed that \"latest available version\" is a good default to track. I still kind of think so, but it does come with problems (not all plugins use semver tags and old Vim plugins might have severely outdated tags; so default branch might make more sense).\n\nI am yet to decide how I'll treat 'mini.deps' when `vim.pack` takes shape. The latter already has several approaches that I wish I'd have in 'mini.deps' (like version ranges; built-in LSP for confirmation buffer plus a more compact text).\n\nThe current standing is that I'll focus on getting `vim.pack` to the shape I want it to have and decide from there:\n- If the final result is enough for me to recommend it over 'mini.deps', I'll just \"retire\" it. It will be around until 'mini.nvim' has support for Neovim=0.11 (so for a while), but on Neovim>=0.12 I'd recommend to use `vim.pack`. In the end, 'mini.deps' was from the start designed with a dream to be upstreamed into core.\n- If not and it won't ever be (like core devs are not fond of some approaches, already present or future), then I might update 'mini.deps' with cherry-picking the best parts of `vim.pack`. \n\nCurrently I think the first scenario is pretty likely. So I don't think spending time on it now delivers the best value, as I'd better focus on improving `vim.pack` instead (it will be inevitably slower, as requires discussions/iterations/etc.).",
            "created_at": "2025-07-05T16:38:00Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1888#issuecomment-3039322647",
